    "body": "## Purpose\n\nOn XPU, FP8 linear layers always run weight-only (W8A16), even on GPUs that can run FP8 GEMMs natively.\n\n`_POSSIBLE_FP8_KERNELS[PlatformEnum.XPU]` lists `XPUW8A16FP8LinearKernel` before `XPUW8A8FP8LinearKernel`, and W8A16 accepts every FP8 layer. W8A16 converts the FP8 weights to 16-bit and runs a 16-bit GEMM. That is the right default for XPUs without FP8 matrix engines. Xe3p GPUs (Crescent Island, Nova Lake-P) have native FP8, so there the W8A8 kernel (`_xpu_C::fp8_gemm`, FP8 activations and weights) is faster. Today the only way to get it is `VLLM_DISABLED_KERNELS=XPUW8A16FP8LinearKernel`.\n\nThis PR makes `init_fp8_linear_kernel` prefer the native kernel. When all of these hold:\n- the platform is XPU,\n- `--linear-backend` is `auto`,\n- the device is Xe3p, detected with `torch.ops._xpu_C.is_xe3p_arch()` from vllm-xpu-kernels,\n\nit passes `force_kernel=XPUW8A8FP8LinearKernel`, the same hook ROCm uses for its batch-invariant FP8 kernel. If W8A8 cannot implement the layer, or is disabled with `VLLM_DISABLED_KERNELS`, selection falls back to the usual list order, which starts with W8A16.\n\nNot affected:\n- XPUs without native FP8 (e.g. Arc B-series): `is_xe3p_arch()` is false, so W8A16 stays the default.\n- vllm-xpu-kernels builds without `is_xe3p_arch` (the op was added in vllm-project/vllm-xpu-kernels@f9f74e4, after the pinned 0.1.15.4): treated as no native FP8, so behaviour is unchanged until the pin moves.\n- Other platforms: the change is in the XPU kernel only.\n- Layers W8A8 cannot implement still get W8A16.\n- An explicit `--linear-backend` (e.g. `xpu_woq`) is respected.\n\n## Test Plan\n\nOn an Xe3p GPU, serve an FP8 online-quantized model with plain `--quantization fp8` and no `VLLM_DISABLED_KERNELS`, then check which kernel the log reports as selected. Run once with `is_xe3p_arch` available and once without it, to cover older vllm-xpu-kernels builds.\n\nThe test machine had a pre-release software stack, so the patch was applied to vLLM v0.32.0. The hunk is identical on `main`. Its vllm-xpu-kernels build exposes `_xpu_C::is_cri` but not `is_xe3p_arch`; for the first run, a test-only `torch.library` shim registered `is_xe3p_arch` as `is_cri`. Both ops were added together upstream.\n\n```bash\nvllm serve nvidia/Cosmos3-Nano --omni --quantization fp8 --cache-backend sea_cache --no-guardrails\n```\n\n## Test Result\n\nKernel selection, `init_fp8_linear_kernel` for a per-tensor and a per-token dynamic FP8 layer (both gave the same result):\n\n| `is_xe3p_arch` | `--linear-backend` | `VLLM_DISABLED_KERNELS` | Selected |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| true | `auto` | - | `XPUW8A8FP8LinearKernel` |\n| true | `xpu_woq` | - | `XPUW8A16FP8LinearKernel` |\n| true | `auto` | `XPUW8A8FP8LinearKernel` | `XPUW8A16FP8LinearKernel` |\n| missing | `auto` | - | `XPUW8A16FP8LinearKernel` (unchanged) |\n\nEnd to end, the Cosmos3-Nano server log reports `Selected XPUW8A8FP8LinearKernel for Fp8PerTensorOnlineLinearMethod` with `is_xe3p_arch` available, and `XPUW8A16FP8LinearKernel` without it. The text-to-video outputs are bit-identical to the existing paths: with this PR they match a run with `VLLM_DISABLED_KERNELS=XPUW8A16FP8LinearKernel`, and without the op they match an unpatched run.\n\nThe generated images and videos were valid in both runs. With W8A8, video generation got noticeably faster; text-to-image was unchanged, because that request is host-bound. I left out absolute numbers because the hardware is pre-release.\n\nI haven't run the XPU CI kernel tests on Arc B-series. On those GPUs, the new branch is skipped because `is_xe3p_arch()` is false.\n\nThe change was co-developed with Claude Code.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
